@X0B
WHAT IS A PCBOARD.DAT?
----------------------
@X03PCBoard.DAT is the primary configuration data file that PCBoard reads, and
which contains the path and filename of all other configuration files.
When using multiple nodes, there is the dilemma that the comm port information
is contained in this file, and it becomes necessary to use one PCBOARD.DAT file
for each separate node.  However, it is possible to use just ONE pcboard.dat
file and run multiple nodes on separate comm ports.

@X0BSETTING UP MULTIPLE NODES
-------------------------
@X03Each node needs a separate and unique subdirectory in order to run.  This
directory will at some point contain temporary files with information that
is specific to that node.  All nodes should be using this directory as the
current subdirectory when the node is launched.  When using multiple pcboard.dat
files, this is where the pcboard.dat file is placed.

When using a single pcboard.dat file, the file should reside in the \PCB
directory.

In PCBSetup, select MODEM INFORMATION, then select MODEM SETUP.  This
information will be ignored when sharing a single pcboard.dat file if you
follow the information provided in this document under the header SETTING
ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES.
 
In PCBSetup, select NODE CONFIGURATION.  Highlight the field labeled
 
RUNNING A NETWORK/MULTITASKER SYSTEM:
 
Enter a <Y> and press ESCape twice to save.  Enter <Q> to save only changes
made.
 
@X0BSETTING ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
-----------------------------
@X03Comm port and node information, among other things, can be specified in the PCB
environment variable.  You can specify an environment variable from a batch
file or dos prompt/OS2 propmpt.  The command is:

SET PCB= [param / data /content]

An example under Os/2 might be:
 
SET PCB=/NODE:1 /PORT1: /BAUD:38400 /BASE:3F8 /IRQ:4 /OS

An example under DOS might be:

SET PCB=/NODE:1 /COM1: /BAUD:38400 /F

Valid parameters for this environment variable are specified in your manual.
PCBoard will look at the com port information specified here before looking
at the PCBoard.DAT file.











 
